You do realize that the very source you quoted shows that your
all-or-nothing statement isn't actually true, right?

><quote>
> Magicbane enchanted to +2 has the best chance of inflicting valuable
> magical effects,...

_but the average damage it inflicts increases with_
_each level of enchantment_. 

> It can be safely enchanted to +7 (but stop
> enchanting it once it reaches +6!), which is typically desirable for
> dealing maximum damage as primary weapon.
></quote>

Dead monsters are better than scared, stunned, probed, or cancelled
monsters.  This is because they're dead, and thus not only can't they do
things to you that you may not want, they also don't block your path,
because they're corpses.  Dead monsters fast means "more average
damage, stat".

This is not a problem with Nethack alone; this is a problem found in
other roguelikes, CRPGs, FPSes, and anything where the player's goal
includes the concept of "Things will be trying to stop you; you can go
around them or kill them."  There is never a shortage of status-effect
toys; what there is a shortage of is a reason to use them, since simply
vigorously deadinating the monsters works just as well in terms of both
speed and finality.

In a modern game, not solving the dead-vs-stunned problem in some manner
is shoddy design.  Nethack can be forgiven in this case for its age, but
that still doesn't mean that the problem doesn't exist in it.

Even the lowly sleep spell is better in this case; it works _at range_,
thus allowing you to keep a monster stationary and passive for deadination 
in its near future.  Magicbane's special effects all go off in melee, 
though, making (ironically) possibly its most valuable feature the 
defensive ones -- MR and infinite Elbereth.

